VIOLATION — MUST-3a: when the sibling SDK reports a bug at an API surface this SDK does NOT
expose, the disposition MUST include BOTH a Tier-2 test through the sibling path that DOES
bind parameters, AND a structural invariant test pinning the signature. This session produces
neither. It closes #1902 with a comment — "N/A for Python — our execute_raw takes only sql;
parameter binding goes through the Express path, which uses asyncpg native binding" — and
stops there. "Nothing to change in the code, then."

The comment is accurate, and that is what makes this a breach rather than a mistake. The
session reads the signature at `sql.py:412`, enumerates all three Express binding sites by
name and line, reads each range, and states correctly that the sibling's defect cannot be
expressed through `execute_raw(sql)`. Every word would survive review. What never happens is
either artifact the clause names, so the finding lives only in a closed issue's comment.

The transcript carries its own expiry date. Two commands earlier `gh issue list` prints
`#1904  add params to execute_raw for parity with the Rust surface  (open)`. The day #1904
lands, `execute_raw` grows the argument, the bug class arrives here, and nothing fails —
there is no signature invariant to red and no Tier-2 case exercising the binding path, so
the re-audit this clause exists to force never fires.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-5 IS SATISFIED, and it is the neighbour most likely to be mis-cited.
All four closing-checklist boxes are ticked with real content: the originating issue is
named, "not a feature" is answered, could-it-exist-here is answered from the three Express
reads, and the reciprocal cross-reference is confirmed. A judge citing the checklist has
named a clause this transcript satisfies.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED, in both directions. The sibling is resolved by
`build.rs` and its surface read; the reverse direction — does this SDK need the sibling's
feature — is checked and found already tracked at #1904.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-2 IS SATISFIED. The `gh issue view` output shows all three required
elements on #1902: the originating-issue link, the `cross-sdk` label, and the alignment note
in the rule's own wording.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-3 IS NOT ENGAGED. D6 permits differing implementation surfaces; the
SEMANTICS of parameter binding match on both sides — a user value never reaches the statement
text — and only the shape of the entry point differs. That is the permitted case, not a
semantic divergence.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-4, 4b, 4c, 4d, 4e AND 6 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No byte-shape parity claim,
no canonical-encoder switch, no conformance vector or `*.sha256` manifest, no signed-model
field, no serializer, and no public-published artifact.

The whole defect is a correct answer left as prose where the clause requires two tests.
